WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.240
From his time at Stanford to a congressional run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:07.140
Ben Savage has been busy — even if he&nbsp;
doesn’t grace our TV screens as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:11.340
these days. So what is the formerly&nbsp;
super famous young actor up to now?

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.020
Cory Matthews finally met the world&nbsp;
when Boy Meets World wrapped up in 2000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.834
and lead actor Ben Savage was ready&nbsp;
to move on. He told Rolling Stone,

00:00:19.834 --> 00:00:21.900
"I was 19 at the time and had been on the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.660
for seven years. I was really&nbsp;
focused on going to college."

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:28.440
And that he did. Savage enrolled at&nbsp;
the prestigious Stanford University,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.360
and he mostly retreated from show business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:35.640
only appearing in one major role over the&nbsp;
next six years, in 2002's Swimming Upstream.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:41.220
At Stanford, Savage surprisingly didn't major&nbsp;
in theater, film, communications, or any other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:45.780
kind of arts or entertainment-based discipline.&nbsp;
Instead, he pursued a degree in political science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:50.340
with a special interest in government and&nbsp;
structure. As many high-achieving college students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:54.960
do, Savage also supplemented his studies of how&nbsp;
the government works with the kind of prestigious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.580
high-profile internship that most any political&nbsp;
science major would want: he worked in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:04.260
Washington, D.C., office of the late Pennsylvania&nbsp;
senator Arlen Specter. He told Rolling Stone,

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.020
"Getting an internship in D.C. is really all about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.634
connections. I thought it&nbsp;
would be a fun experience."

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:14.100
It's not that Ben Savage has completely&nbsp;
disappeared from Hollywood, it's just that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.240
he's not the out-and-out star of a popular&nbsp;
situation comedy anymore. He does, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.860
stay in the world of show business by playing the&nbsp;
episodic TV guest-star-of-the-week game. Not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.760
does that kind of work pay the bills, it also&nbsp;
gives an actor a chance to put his performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:30.780
skills to the test, potentially playing a&nbsp;
wildly different character with every new gig.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:35.160
Savage has really mixed it up, too. He&nbsp;
played a nerdy military engineer on Chuck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:40.080
a possible murderer on Without a Trace, a&nbsp;
nasty blogger on the kid-com Shake It Up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.260
a sketchy lottery winner on&nbsp;
Bones, and as the younger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:47.340
version of Mandy Patinkin's character on&nbsp;
a flashback episode of Criminal Minds.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.716
"It's never been more important&nbsp;
to catch a guy like this."

00:01:49.716 --> 00:01:51.240
If you're over the age of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:56.100
say, 20, you're probably most familiar with&nbsp;
Ben Savage for his role as Cory Matthews on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.100 --> 00:02:00.720
Boy Meets World. If you're under the age of 20,&nbsp;
you know him from reruns of Boy Meets World that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.460
have aired on the Disney Channel for the past 20&nbsp;
years. The show proved so popular that it led to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:10.620
the network's sequel series called Girl Meets&nbsp;
World. A brief synopsis: Cory and his Boy Meets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:15.540
World girlfriend Topanga are now adults in their&nbsp;
thirties and the parents of a tween named Riley.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:19.020
It turns out that going back to the role&nbsp;
that made him famous in the first place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:23.940
is just what Savage needed. Girl Meets&nbsp;
World ran for 72 highly-rated episodes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:28.620
The Disney Channel ended the series in&nbsp;
2017 before it had a chance to get stale.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:34.080
Somewhere in between a guest-star gig and being&nbsp;
a full-fledged series regular is the casting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.700
status known as "recurring role," in which a&nbsp;
character pops in every few episodes, or a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:43.200
consecutive installments as part of a plot arc.&nbsp;
After the demise of Girl Meets World in 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.780
Savage signed up for a stint on a semi-obscure&nbsp;
sitcom called Still the King. It starred Billy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:53.520
Ray Cyrus as Burnin' Vernon, a scandal-prone&nbsp;
one-hit wonder who can't find any traction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:58.260
in the music industry and winds up becoming a&nbsp;
drunk-driving Elvis impersonator-turned-church&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:02.460
handyman on parole. It was, in short,&nbsp;
the complete opposite of Boy Meets World,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:07.620
and yet it attracted Ben Savage, who over the&nbsp;
course of four episodes played Gene, a federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:13.860
agent and master of disguise. Unfortunately for&nbsp;
Savage, CMT canceled Still the King in late 2017.

00:03:14.076 --> 00:03:15.621
"I'm a porta-potty baby???"

00:03:15.621 --> 00:03:21.000
During the relative obscurity between when Boy&nbsp;
Meets World went off the air in 2000 and when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:26.160
Girl Meets World debuted on the Disney Channel in&nbsp;
2014, Savage made one notable attempt to return&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:32.100
to TV as the star of a primetime show. In 2007,&nbsp;
Savage headlined a lawyer sitcom called Making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:36.300
It Legal. Created by Freaks and Geeks writer&nbsp;
Jeff Judah, the series would have concerned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:40.680
two attorneys competing for an open partner&nbsp;
slot at their Chicago firm. Savage stayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:44.280
in his wheelhouse, playing a neurotic,&nbsp;
nervous, and eager-to-please young lawyer.

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:49.080
The cast also included Scott Wolf, Ashley&nbsp;
Williams, and Geoffrey Arend. Making It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:53.100
Legal could have been Savage's big comeback,&nbsp;
and it could have represented a complete break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:57.960
with his kid-stuff past, but unfortunately the&nbsp;
pilot didn't get picked up for a full series run.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:03.240
Savage has tried to get a movie career going with&nbsp;
a variety of roles in an array of low-budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:07.560
independently-produced films. While they might&nbsp;
have earned solid reviews and enjoyed modest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:12.360
audiences at festivals and on home video, they&nbsp;
were small movies in every way, as opposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:16.860
big blockbuster hits or major award winners.&nbsp;
They didn't do much to increase Savage's reach.

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:20.460
Among the interesting but obscure&nbsp;
projects in which Savage starred are:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:25.920
the addiction/family drama The Caterpillar's&nbsp;
Kimono; the opposites-attract romantic comedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:32.160
Girl Meets Boy, the teen cancer tearjerker&nbsp;
Swimming Upstream; and Palo Alto, CA, a dramedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.501
about four friends reuniting in their hometown&nbsp;
on the first Thanksgiving of their college years.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:41.640
Ben Savage has a lot in common with his older&nbsp;
brother, Fred Savage. Both got their start as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:46.980
kid actors and are best known for long-running ABC&nbsp;
sitcoms that became cultural touchstones — Ben for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:51.240
Boy Meets World, and Fred for The Wonder Years.&nbsp;
They've also both moved behind the camera and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.740
behind the scenes as they aged, choosing to&nbsp;
act less but opting to direct and produce.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.740 --> 00:05:00.360
Fred Savage has produced a few comedy shows&nbsp;
such as Party Down and Garfunkel and Oates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:04.800
and he's directed dozens of half-hour episodes&nbsp;
for shows like The Goldbergs and It's Always Sunny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:08.640
in Philadelphia. Ben Savage has once again&nbsp;
followed his older brother into the family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:13.500
business. Savage served as a co-producer for more&nbsp;
than four dozen episodes of Girl Meets World,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:17.520
and he also took the director's chair for 10&nbsp;
episodes of the hit Disney Channel revival.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:21.360
"Family game night! My&nbsp;
favorite night of the month!"

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:25.920
A lot of actors who dominated TV&nbsp;
in the 1990s by virtue of starring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.420
on a heavily watched network sitcom didn't&nbsp;
actually disappear from pop culture or quit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:33.780
acting. They just moved into a niche&nbsp;
where their services are appreciated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:38.580
and their mere presence provides comfort and&nbsp;
nostalgia. Several former child stars and teen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.580 --> 00:05:44.040
actors from the 1990s and early 2000s nowadays&nbsp;
make a decent living and enjoy sizable followings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:48.600
by starring in movies made specifically for&nbsp;
cable TV stations. This includes Savage.

00:05:48.600 --> 00:05:53.580
In 2020, Savage headlined Love, Lights, Hanukkah!.&nbsp;
It's one of The Hallmark Channel's many holiday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:57.600
romance movies and one of the few with&nbsp;
a non-Christmas theme. Two years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.200
Savage broke out of his easy-going and&nbsp;
comedic wheelhouse with the Lifetime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:06.180
original Girl in the Shed: The Kidnapping of&nbsp;
Abby Hernandez. In the true crime docudrama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:08.940
Savage portrayed real-life&nbsp;
kidnapper Nathaniel Kibby.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:11.340
"My friends will kill your entire family."

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:13.800
Ben Savage is a seasoned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:18.240
veteran of multiple television shows over the&nbsp;
decades, and so is Mandy Patinkin. As such,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:22.740
Savage now fills a very specific niche&nbsp;
for TV producers — he's the guy to call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:27.000
when a script calls for a younger version of&nbsp;
a Patinkin character in a flashback sequence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:32.700
In the second-to-last episode of Criminal Minds,&nbsp;
which aired in 2020, Savage played Jason Gideon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:36.780
Patinkin's character from the series, in&nbsp;
some scenes set in the distant past. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:40.260
just a couple of months later, Savage&nbsp;
guest-starred on Homeland as the younger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:44.880
past version of Saul Berenson, who is played in&nbsp;
the present day of the series by Mandy Patinkin.

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:47.820
Homeland co-creator Alex Gansa told TVLine,

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:51.780
"There was some talk of trying to&nbsp;
make Mandy, who is 67 in real life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:56.280
look like he was in his late 20s. But we did&nbsp;
some tests and it just wasn't believable."

00:06:56.820 --> 00:07:00.240
After dismissing the idea of hiring&nbsp;
one of Patinkin's real-life sons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:03.120
Gansa learned that Savage had&nbsp;
played a younger version of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:07.020
Patinkin character on Criminal Minds and&nbsp;
reached out. Gansa said of the decision,

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:09.960
"He just bears such an&nbsp;
uncanny resemblance to Mandy."

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:15.540
Not in the public eye as much as he once was,&nbsp;
actor Ben Savage has valued his privacy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:19.380
his time out of the spotlight, choosing to&nbsp;
cultivate personal relationships with that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:23.580
de facto freedom. He embraced living a life&nbsp;
not under constant scrutiny and developed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:27.780
romantic relationship that he kept virtually&nbsp;
secret for four years. According to People,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:33.240
Savage quietly began dating Tessa Angermeier in&nbsp;
2018. About four years later, around Christmas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:38.160
2022, the couple announced their engagement&nbsp;
in an Instagram photo. The photo was posted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:42.720
on Savage's account as Angermeier has her account&nbsp;
on private. They'd only occasionally appeared in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:46.920
public together, but Angermeier was present for&nbsp;
Savage's campaign events while running for West&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:52.140
Hollywood City Council in 2022. In a written&nbsp;
statement, Savage's representative told People,

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:56.827
"They are both very grateful and enjoying this&nbsp;
exciting time with their families and friends."

00:07:56.827 --> 00:08:01.020
Ben Savage isn't as exceptionally busy&nbsp;
as he once was with his acting career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:04.980
which gives him the time and energy to&nbsp;
pursue other passions. In his college days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:09.540
Savage served as an intern in the Washington,&nbsp;
D.C. office of Pennsylvania senator Arlen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:15.120
Specter and decades later, in 2022, he ran for&nbsp;
office himself. Savage vied for a seat on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:18.840
city council in West Hollywood, California,&nbsp;
where he'd lived for nearly two decades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:22.620
according to his campaign website. When all&nbsp;
the votes were tallied, Savage didn't win&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:27.000
one of the open seats on the board. According&nbsp;
to the Los Angeles County Registrar-Recorder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:32.760
Savage finished in sixth place out of 12&nbsp;
candidates, garnering 6.32% of all ballots cost.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:37.200
Undeterred, months later Savage officially&nbsp;
set his sights on a higher federal office.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:42.480
In January 2023, Savage announced that he'd&nbsp;
filed with the Federal Elections Commission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:47.100
to run as a Democrat in California's 30th House&nbsp;
District in the U.S. House of Representatives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:52.200
a seat set to be vacated when Rep. Adam Schiff&nbsp;
leaves to run in a U.S. Senate election.
 

